1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,960
Good evening everyone and welcome to Film Strip.

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:05,760
Tonight we go behind the scenes on the new movie,

3
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:09,300
The Forger, which will be in cinemas later this year.

4
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:11,900
The Forger is a remarkable true story

5
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:15,880
about one of the biggest art forgery crimes in history.

6
00:00:15,880 --> 00:00:18,680
The hero of the film is John Myatt.

7
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:21,560
When the film starts, Myatt is a school teacher

8
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:24,200
in Staffordshire in central England.

9
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:26,200
Myatt is a frustrated artist.

10
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:28,640
He went to art school, but was never able

11
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:30,920
to make enough money from his painting.

12
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:33,880
One day, he comes home early to find his wife

13
00:00:33,880 --> 00:00:35,280
with another man.

14
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:37,560
She leaves him and their two children,

15
00:00:37,560 --> 00:00:41,800
and Myatt soon finds himself overworked and broke.

16
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:43,920
Now, Myatt is not a great painter,

17
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:45,920
but he's a brilliant imitator.

18
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:49,880
He can copy the paintings and styles of famous artists.

19
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:52,160
So he puts an ad in a magazine.

20
00:00:52,160 --> 00:00:54,040
Let's hear the clip.

21
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:55,400
- Yes, hello.

22
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:59,280
I'd like to put an advertisement in next week's issue.

23
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:03,920
It reads, "Genuine fakes from 150 pounds."

24
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:05,240
Yes, that's all.

25
00:01:05,240 --> 00:01:07,240
And my phone number.

26
00:01:07,240 --> 00:01:10,080
- There's nothing illegal about copying art.

27
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:12,400
A genuine fake, as Myatt called it,

28
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:14,280
as long as you sign it yourself

29
00:01:14,280 --> 00:01:16,400
and don't pretend that it was really painted

30
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:18,160
by someone else.

31
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:21,040
Myatt's business was doing well for a few years.

32
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:24,600
He copied paintings by Monet, Clay, Nicholson,

33
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:26,760
famous 20th century artists,

34
00:01:26,760 --> 00:01:29,560
and he was able to spend more time with his children,

35
00:01:29,560 --> 00:01:31,240
Amy and Sam.

36
00:01:31,240 --> 00:01:34,320
One of his best customers was an art collector in London

37
00:01:34,320 --> 00:01:36,440
called John Drew.

38
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:38,920
Drew had bought many genuine fakes from Myatt,

39
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:41,440
and the two Johns had become friends.

40
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:44,720
Then one day, Myatt received a phone call from Drew

41
00:01:44,720 --> 00:01:47,640
that changed both of their lives.

42
00:01:47,640 --> 00:01:51,440
- John, do you remember the copy of the Albert Glaze painting

43
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:52,960
that you did for me recently?

44
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:54,280
- Yes, of course.

45
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:55,920
It took me a long time.

46
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:58,240
I thought it was rather good.

47
00:01:58,240 --> 00:01:59,880
- You're not the only one.

48
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:03,360
Someone at Christie's yesterday thought it was very good.

49
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:04,760
- Christie's?

50
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:06,720
The auction house in London?

51
00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:08,960
That's where real paintings are bought and sold,

52
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:10,200
not my fakes.

53
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:12,680
- Not anymore, my friend.

54
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:17,680
I sold your glaze as a genuine glaze for 25,000 pounds.

55
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:21,400
- You did what?

56
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:23,400
But they'll find out it's a fake.

57
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:24,640
(John laughs)

58
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:26,960
- No, they won't, John.

59
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:30,560
I wrote some documents to give the painting a history.

60
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:33,800
Old paper, old typewriter, who cares?

61
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:35,560
The buyer likes the painting.

62
00:02:35,560 --> 00:02:37,600
He thinks he has an original,

63
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:41,920
and you've earned 12,500 pounds.

64
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:43,320
Come to London.

65
00:02:43,320 --> 00:02:46,080
I'll give you your money, cash, of course,

66
00:02:46,080 --> 00:02:49,800
and we can discuss our next project.

67
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:51,960
- I don't know, John.

68
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:54,040
This can't be legal.

69
00:02:54,040 --> 00:02:57,120
- How much do you earn as a teacher, John?

70
00:02:57,120 --> 00:02:59,600
- About 13,000.

71
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:03,440
- You've just nearly doubled your salary.

72
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:06,320
- How can Mayotte resist an offer like that?

73
00:03:06,320 --> 00:03:10,000
Almost $25,000 for a forgery?

74
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:10,960
He can't.

75
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:12,640
And over the next few years,

76
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:16,360
Drew and Mayotte sell one million pounds.

77
00:03:16,360 --> 00:03:19,600
That's nearly $2 million of fake art

78
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:22,720
to dealers and museums all over the world,

79
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:27,720
including a Giacometti which sold for $300,000 in New York.

80
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:31,760
Drew is the brains behind the operation.

81
00:03:31,760 --> 00:03:33,800
At one point, he tells Mayotte

82
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:36,120
that he's not doing it for the money.

83
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:38,920
For Drew, forgery is a game.

84
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:42,160
He wants to trick people into paying for a fake

85
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:44,160
as if it were genuine.

86
00:03:44,160 --> 00:03:47,480
He goes to extraordinary lengths in his deception.

87
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:51,400
For example, he tricks his way into two top art museums

88
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:53,320
in London and leaves photographs

89
00:03:53,320 --> 00:03:56,200
of the fake paintings in old catalogs.

90
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:58,640
Then when he's auctioning the painting,

91
00:03:58,640 --> 00:04:03,160
he can go to the museum and prove that it's the real thing.

92
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:05,840
But of course, it has to end.

93
00:04:05,840 --> 00:04:09,880
You might remember the court trial in the late 1990s.

94
00:04:09,880 --> 00:04:12,040
Drew was so good at forgery

95
00:04:12,040 --> 00:04:15,800
that he faked two heart attacks to delay the trial.

96
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:20,400
Eventually though, both Drew and Mayotte were sent to prison.

97
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:22,600
Drew was there for six years,

98
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:24,640
but Mayotte cooperated with the police

99
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:27,940
and he was only imprisoned for four months.

100
00:04:27,940 --> 00:04:30,360
The judge recognized that Drew was the one

101
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:32,840
who was really committing the forgery.

102
00:04:32,840 --> 00:04:34,980
I won't tell you the twist to the story

103
00:04:34,980 --> 00:04:37,080
after Mayotte gets out of prison.

104
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:40,700
It's almost too good to be true, but it is.

105
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:43,720
We will tell you that Mayotte became more famous

106
00:04:43,720 --> 00:04:45,640
because of all of the publicity

107
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:49,480
and that he now sells his genuine fakes, properly labeled,

108
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:52,680
for up to $10,000.

109
00:04:52,680 --> 00:04:55,500
How much money did Mayotte make for his forgeries?

110
00:04:55,500 --> 00:05:00,500
About $26,000 a year, about the same as his teaching salary.

111
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:04,800
The forgery hits the big screen later this year.

